1.WHICH AI TOOLS YOU USED
I utilized Google Gemini as an AI assistant throughout this analysis.

2.WHAT YOU USED THEM FOR
Sentence Rephrasing & Professional Documentation: Polishing raw business insights into clear, structured, and formal report-style documentation.
Query Syntax Optimization & Debugging: Identifying PostgreSQL syntax requirements, such as explicit data type casting (e.g., using ::NUMERIC for rounding functions) and date-parsing functions.
Schema & Data Gap Evaluation: Brainstorming missing data entities (such as marketing ad spend, product cost/margins, and unique customer IDs) to evaluate dataset limitations and analytical risks.

3.ONE EXAMPLE WHERE AI HELPED YOU
AI helped streamline the creation of complex PostgreSQL queries, specifically around time-series logic. 
It provided the structural syntax to extract months and calculate percentage growth between peak months (Q4) and
baseline yearly averages using Common Table Expressions (CTEs).

4.ONE EXAMPLE WHERE YOU HAD TO VERIFY, CORRECT, OR MODIFY AN AI-GENERATED RESULT
When generating date-parsing queries for the Transaction_Date column, the AI initially suggested using string conversion functions like TO_DATE() or TO_TIMESTAMP(). 
However, upon testing the query on my local PostgreSQL environment, the database threw a type error (function to_timestamp(date, unknown) does not exist).
I recognized that PostgreSQL had automatically imported my CSV date column as a native DATE type rather than a text string. I corrected the code by removing
the redundant conversion wrapper and applying EXTRACT(MONTH FROM Transaction_Date) directly. Additionally, because AI models do not have direct access to local 
database values, I independently verified and replaced all placeholder metrics with the actual values calculated from my execution of the SQL queries on the dataset.
